"""Checkpoint closure, strict loader, local commits, a file-backed versioned store and the publish/restore protocol.

PLAN §6.6 (checkpoint closure, publish protocol steps 1-3, cadence, restore proofs), §6.8 (at-resume rules) and
§5 P7 tests 4-6.

- Closure files are safetensors (``metadata=None``) for tensors and canonical JSON for everything else. Nothing is
  pickled and ``torch.load`` is never called.
- A local commit is one immutable directory ``<commits_root>/<step:010d>/`` holding the closure files plus
  ``commit.json``. It is built in a temporary sibling directory and renamed into place.
- ``read_commit`` is strict. Every listed file must be present with equal sha256 and size, and no other file may be
  present. Tensor files must be in canonical safetensors form, JSON files must be canonical, and the tensor inventories
  and ``parameter_sha256`` are recomputed. Any failure raises ``CheckpointRefused``.
- ``FileVersionedStore`` is a local stand-in with S3 conditional-write semantics: a create-only PUT answers 412 when
  the key exists, an If-Match PUT answers 412 when the ETag is stale, and every version can be read back exactly.
  It has no delete.
- Publishing follows §6.6: dependencies, then the manifest (both create-only, with an exact-version readback), then the
  resume pointer by compare-and-swap against the persisted expected state. A create-only key holding different
  bytes is ``PoisonedKey``; an unresolvable pointer state is ``Conflict``. Both are terminal for a run.
- ``resume_point`` applies the §6.8 at-resume rules (continue from the newest local commit when it is the pointer
  target, publish it first when it is the target's child, restore the target when it has no local copy, otherwise
  STOP), so a local commit is never discarded silently. The pointer's writer block carries ``event_seq``, the
  writer's event high-water mark, and a resume continues above it (§6.6 Events).
"""
